**Exercise 8 (S).** Decide which of the following sets are subspaces (with proof or counterexample).

(a) $U_1 = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}^3 \mid x_1 + x_2 = x_3 \} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^3$;

(b) $U_2 = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}^3 \mid x_1 x_2 = 0 \} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^3$;

(c) $U_3 = \{ f \in \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}} \mid f(1) = 2f(0) \} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}}$;

(d) $U_4 = \{ f \in \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}} \mid f(t) \geq 0 \text{ for all } t \in \mathbb{R} \} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{R}}$.

## 4.2 Subspaces

**Definition 4.2.1.** A subset $U$ of a $K$-vector space $V$ is a *(linear) subspace* if

- (U1) $0 \in U$;
- (U2) $u, u' \in U \Rightarrow u + u' \in U$;
- (U3) $\lambda \in K$, $u \in U \Rightarrow \lambda u \in U$.

## Theorem 4.3.7 (Characterisation of bases)

*For a family $\mathcal{B} = (v_1, \ldots, v_n)$ in $V$ the following are equivalent:*:

*(i)* $\mathcal{B}$ is a basis of $V$;

*(ii)* $\mathcal{B}$ is a minimal *generating family*: it generates $V$, but no proper subfamily does;

*(iii)* $\mathcal{B}$ is a maximal *linearly independent family*: it is linearly independent, but $(v_1, \ldots, v_n, v)$ is dependent for every $v \in V$;

*(iv)* every $v \in V$ can be written in a unique way as $v = \lambda_1 v_1 + \cdots + \lambda_n v_n$.
